WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Two numbers on your DiaBite receipt can look
alike, but they answer different questions.

2
00:00:03.847 --> 00:00:08.147
The glycemic index, or GI, tells you how fast a
food raises blood sugar.

3
00:00:08.529 --> 00:00:10.814
But GI says nothing about how much you eat.

4
00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:14.941
That is what glycemic load, or GL, adds: the index
and the portion together.

5
00:00:14.991 --> 00:00:17.059
It tells you how much a meal raises it.

6
00:00:17.132 --> 00:00:20.039
Here is one slice of rye bread, thirty-five grams.

7
00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:23.055
Fourteen point two grams of available
carbohydrate.

8
00:00:23.105 --> 00:00:23.588
GI fifty.

9
00:00:23.638 --> 00:00:25.362
Glycemic load seven point one.

10
00:00:25.437 --> 00:00:27.023
Now two slices, seventy grams.

11
00:00:27.073 --> 00:00:28.387
The index is still fifty.

12
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:30.787
But the load doubles, to fourteen point two.

13
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:33.505
Same bread, same GI, twice the effect.

14
00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:35.489
That is why the portion matters.

15
00:00:35.581 --> 00:00:39.613
On the receipt, read each food's portion and
available carbohydrate first,

16
00:00:39.663 --> 00:00:41.103
then its GI, then its load.

17
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:43.861
The load is the figure that adds up across a meal.

18
00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:45.543
DiaBite is a reference tool.

19
00:00:45.593 --> 00:00:47.504
It does not replace your care team.
